Online Shopping Purchase Prediction
Overview

This project predicts whether a visitor on an online shopping website will make a purchase during a browsing session. The prediction is based on user behavior such as how many pages they visit, how much time they spend on the website, and whether they are a returning customer.

This project was created as part of an Artificial Intelligence assignment, based on the CS50 AI – Shopping problem.

The focus of this project is not just building a model, but also understanding how well it performs using meaningful evaluation metrics.

What This Project Does

Analyzes user activity from an online shopping dataset

Trains a machine learning model to predict purchase intent

Evaluates results using sensitivity and specificity

Prints clear and understandable results in the terminal

Dataset

File: shopping.csv

Around 12,000 user sessions

Each row represents a single visit to the website

Features include:

Number of pages visited (administrative, informational, product-related)

Time spent on different pages

Bounce rate and exit rate

Month of visit

Browser, region, and operating system

Visitor type (new or returning)

Weekend or weekday visit

Target Column

Revenue

TRUE → Purchase made

FALSE → No purchase

The dataset is used as provided and is not altered.

Machine Learning Model

Algorithm: K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)

k value: 1

Library: scikit-learn

Why KNN?

KNN with k = 1 was chosen because it is simple, easy to understand, and follows the assignment requirements. The model predicts outcomes by comparing each session with the most similar past session.

How the Program Works

The dataset file is passed through the command line

Data is loaded and converted into numerical form

The dataset is split into:

60% training data

40% testing data

A KNN classifier is trained using the training data

Predictions are made on the test data

Sensitivity and specificity are calculated manually

Results are displayed in the terminal

How to Run
Requirements

Python 3.10 or higher

scikit-learn

Install dependencies if needed:

pip install scikit-learn


Run the project:

python shopping.py shopping.csv

Sample Output
Correct: 4112
Incorrect: 820
Sensitivity (True Positive Rate): 38.77%
Specificity (True Negative Rate): 91.57%

Notes:

High specificity shows the model is very good at identifying non-purchasing users

Lower sensitivity reflects the challenge of predicting purchases in imbalanced datasets

The project follows the original problem constraints strictly

Final Thoughts

This project is a simple but practical example of using machine learning to analyze user behavior. It highlights both the strengths and limitations of basic models when applied to real-world data.

